# Trusted lines of the Bedrock2 prototype (format: ../count-trusted.sh).
#
# Modelling style: trusted shim. Bedrock2's language calls functions by
# name only (cmd.call); anything else is an external action
# (cmd.interact), whose behaviour the proofs take from ext_spec and
# which ToCString prints as a C call to a function of the action's name.
# The three indirect calls are such actions, made by the C shim. VU.v
# assumes no ext_spec: the contract of each action is a section variable
# of VU.v, so it is a hypothesis of every theorem (Print Assumptions:
# closed under the global context), and the theorems hold for any shim
# and client that meet those contracts.
#
# The shim: three C functions that make the indirect calls and return
# the callee's raw int result as a word; vu_call_apply_all also takes
# the address of the verified vu_i_apply (re-entrancy shape (a)).
shim.c
# The word-level C names of the actions and of the verified functions
# (hand-written ABI mapping; the compiler checks the generated
# definitions against it).
b2ext.h
# The proto.h wrappers: int to word conversions (sign or zero
# extension), word to int truncation.
abi.c
# The struct offsets that the memory predicates assume, written as
# numbers.
layout.c:8-19
# The w notation and the memory predicates the contracts use: the args
# record (whole, with its padding), the subject (whole: Bedrock2 has
# no fractional permission, so no read-only share), the fold record.
VU.v:111-111
VU.v:117-140
# The external world's ext_spec is well formed (ext_spec.ok), which
# bedrock2's program logic needs.
VU.v:164-164
# Contract of the encoding callee (vu_call_enc_len): lent the byte at
# p, gives it back unchanged, returns an int.
VU.v:168-171
# Contract of the callout callee (vu_call_callout): lent the record and
# the subject; gives back the record with subject, subject_len, pos and
# num unchanged (slots and padding any) and the subject with the same
# bytes; returns an int.
VU.v:178-184
# Contract of the client's apply_all (vu_call_apply_all), stated on the
# function it is given: the client preserves every property of the
# record that every call of b2_vu_i_apply preserves (as Isabelle-LLVM's
# respects_i_apply). A hypothesis of b2_vu_fold_all_ok, composed there
# with b2_vu_i_apply_ok (i_apply_rec_wf).
VU.v:192-203
